# Accepted security findings — the documented exception list for the Trivy scan
# in .github/workflows/security-scan.yml.
#
# Same discipline as job-applications' `pip-audit --ignore-vuln`: an exception is
# allowed, but it must be WRITTEN DOWN with a reason and a revisit trigger. A
# silently-suppressed finding is indistinguishable from one nobody noticed.
#
# Format: one finding ID per line. Every entry MUST carry, in a comment above it:
#   - what it is, and why it is accepted (not merely inconvenient)
#   - the compensating control, if any
#   - what would make us revisit
#
# An entry with no reason is not an exception, it is a hole. Prefer upgrading the
# pinned digest over adding a line here.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DS-0002 (HIGH) — "Specify at least 1 USER command with a non-root user".
# Affects claude-runner, gemini-runner and scrubber. router and lanes already run
# non-root (USER node / USER lanes).
#
# Surfaced 2026-07-25, and ONLY because the Dockerfiles were repaired: while the
# FROM lines were unparseable, Trivy's config scanner silently found nothing.
# A scanner with nothing to say is not the same as a clean scan.
#
# WHY IT CANNOT SIMPLY BE FIXED — verified 2026-07-25, not assumed:
#  * scrubber: /vault and /workspace are bind mounts on a Windows drive exposed to
#    WSL over 9p/drvfs, mounted with uid=0;gid=0 SYNTHESIZED and no metadata
#    option. Everything under it reads as root:root and `chown` is a silent no-op
#    (tested). `USER node` (uid 1000) would leave the scrubber unable to write the
#    PII vault at all. This is a platform constraint, not an oversight.
#  * claude-runner / gemini-runner: carry cap_add NET_ADMIN and run
#    init-firewall.sh, which sets iptables at startup and requires root. The real
#    fix is to drop privileges AFTER firewall setup (gosu), which touches the
#    startup path and the CLI auth volumes.
#
# COMPENSATING CONTROL ADDED 2026-07-25 — since the uid cannot change, take away
# what root can DO: scrubber, router and lanes now run with `cap_drop: [ALL]` and
# `no-new-privileges:true`. Verified: CapEff=0000000000000000 in all three, and
# all three still function (scrubber writes vault + workspace and serves; lanes
# /health returns ok; router responds). Root inside a container with zero
# capabilities and no escalation path is a substantially smaller target.
# Plus: all are internal to agentnet with NO published host port, the runners are
# default-deny egress, and the scrubber needs no egress at all.
#
# STILL THE WEAKEST EXCEPTION HERE. Reduced, not resolved.
# REVISIT: (a) move /vault and /workspace to NAMED DOCKER VOLUMES, which Docker
# owns and can chown, making USER node possible — the real fix, and an
# architectural change worth doing deliberately; (b) or enable WSL metadata mounts
# (/etc/wsl.conf), noting that changes ownership semantics for every project on
# that drive; (c) runners — drop-after-firewall with gosu. Monthly review.
DS-0002

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-60002 — OpenSSH: use-after-free during host key exchange.
# Found by the scheduled scan on 2026-09-01/02, in n8nio/n8n (openssh
# 10.3_p1-r0, across 7 sub-packages). Alpine fixed it in 10.3_p1-r1.
#
# WHY ACCEPTED — not merely inconvenient: there is no patched n8n image to move
# to. Checked on 2026-09-03 by resolving and scanning every current tag —
# 2.36.9 (deployed), 2.37.7 and 2.38.3 (published that morning) ALL carry
# 10.3_p1-r0. n8n has not rebuilt against the patched alpine package yet, so
# upgrading is not an available action, and the image is a Docker Hardened Image
# with the package manager removed, so patching it ourselves is not either.
#
# COMPENSATING CONTROLS — the vulnerable code is unreachable in this deployment:
#   - No sshd runs in the container (checked: 0 matching processes). The image
#     ships the openssh package; nothing starts it.
#   - No workflow uses an SSH or Git node. The complete set of node types across
#     all 18 imported workflows is: code, convertToFile, errorTrigger,
#     executeWorkflow, executeWorkflowTrigger, httpRequest, if, manualTrigger,
#     noOp, readWriteFile, scheduleTrigger, set, switch, wait, webhook.
#   - n8n binds 127.0.0.1 and [::1] only, on the internal agentnet.
# The CVE needs an SSH client negotiating with a hostile server, or a server
# accepting connections. Neither code path is invoked here.
#
# REVISIT: at the monthly image-policy review (context/image-policy.json
# reviewBy), check whether any published n8n tag ships openssh >= 10.3_p1-r1;
# when one does, re-pin and DELETE this entry. Revisit IMMEDIATELY if any
# workflow gains an SSH or Git node, or if anything starts sshd in that
# container — either would create the missing reachability.
CVE-2026-60002

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-59873 — node-tar: DoS via a crafted gzip bomb.
# Found by the first real run of this scan (2026-07-25), in n8nio/n8n (tar
# 7.5.16) and the node:22-bookworm-slim base (7.5.11). Fixed upstream in 7.5.19.
#
# RESTORED 2026-09-17 — AND WHY IT WAS EVER DELETED. On 2026-09-03 this entry was
# removed because the re-pinned node digest "shipped tar 7.5.19". It did — but
# that digest was node:latest (Node 26, full Debian 13), taken from a Dependabot
# PR without checking what it pinned, not node:22-bookworm-slim. The exception
# was only unnecessary because the image had silently changed. Re-verified
# 2026-09-17 against node:22-bookworm-slim as resolved that day (Node 22.23.2):
# npm's bundled tar is still 7.5.11, and it is the ONLY CRITICAL Trivy reports.
# n8nio/n8n 2.36.9 does not carry it: the scheduled scan passed with this
# entry absent every night from 2026-09-03 to 2026-09-12.
#
# WHY ACCEPTED — not merely inconvenient: no node:22-bookworm-slim build ships a
# patched npm yet, so upgrading within the pinned major is not an available
# action; moving major version to escape a DoS in a build-time tool would trade a
# contained finding for an untested runtime change across every service.
#
# COMPENSATING CONTROLS: the vulnerable code is npm's bundled tar, used at BUILD
# time from a lockfile, never on untrusted runtime input. The services built on
# this base (router, scrubber, claude-runner, gemini-runner) are HTTP services
# that do not extract archives — re-checked 2026-09-17: no tar/zip/gunzip/extract
# call in router, scrubber, either runner, lanes or any n8n workflow. Severity is
# availability-only — no code execution, no data disclosure.
#
# REVISIT: at the monthly image-policy review (context/image-policy.json
# reviewBy), check whether node:22-bookworm-slim ships tar >= 7.5.19; when it
# does, re-pin and DELETE this entry. Revisit immediately if anything starts
# extracting archives from an external source (e.g. drive-sync handling zipped
# attachments), because that would create the missing reachability.
CVE-2026-59873
